        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Americas</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Mapuche</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">South-central Chile from the Choapa Valley to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiloe">Chiloé Archipelago</a>, the heartland between the Itata and Toltén rivers (Araucanía); later Puelmapu, the Argentine pampa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patagonia">Patagonia</a>; Purén, Lumaco and Budi Lake.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">An archaeological Mapuche culture from c. 600–500 BCE; Arauco War against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from 1550; independent south of the Biobío after 1598; conquered by Chile (1861–1883) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> (1870s); a living people today.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">lineage communities led by lonkos and wartime toquis; the machi, usually a woman healer; the Ngillatun ceremony; textiles and silverwork; three centuries of resistance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arauco-war">Arauco War</a>; the spread of their language and culture east of the Andes</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavated sites such as the Pitrén Cemetery and the raised fields of Budi Lake; Spanish chroniclers and the epic La Araucana; genetic and linguistic studies; Mapuche oral tradition and remembered history; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> records.</span></div>
        </div>
