    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Mapuche</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>More than 2,500 years ago, the Mapuche already lived in the land that is now Chile.</p><p>Their name means “people of the land”, and they live in south-central Chile and south-western Argentina.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maize.jpg" alt="Maize" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Maize <span class="kid-tale-cr">Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Most Mapuche were farmers who grew potatoes, maize and quinoa, and each family had a chief, the lonko.</p><p>A machi, usually a woman, is a healer who holds ceremonies to cure illness and to help the harvests.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lautaro.jpg" alt="Lautaro" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Lautaro <span class="kid-tale-cr">GringoInChile, CC BY 2.5</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 475 years ago Spanish soldiers built forts on Mapuche land, and a war began that lasted nearly 350 years.</p><p>Leaders such as Lautaro won battles, and the land south of the Biobío River stayed free of Spanish rule.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silver.jpg" alt="Silver" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Silver <span class="kid-tale-cr">Alchemist-hp, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</span></figcaption></figure><p>Many Mapuche women still weave ponchos, blankets and belts, as their mothers and grandmothers taught them.</p><p>Mapuche silversmiths melted silver coins to make jewellery and headdresses.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/occupation-of-araucania.jpg" alt="Occupation of Araucanía" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Occupation of Araucanía <span class="kid-tale-cr">Ccpchile, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Later, Chile and Argentina took Mapuche lands by war, and thousands of Mapuche died.</p><p>Today they are the largest Indigenous people of Chile, and they still work for their land, language and rights.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Mapuche lived in farming lineages along the rivers of south-central Chile, led by lonkos in peace and by elected toquis in war, without ever forming a state.</li><li>After 1550 they fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arauco-war">Arauco War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> for nearly 350 years, and from 1598 kept their lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biobio-river">Biobío River</a> free of Spanish rule.</li><li>Chile and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> conquered their lands between 1861 and the 1880s; today the Mapuche are the largest Indigenous people of Chile and struggle for their land and rights.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Mapuche silversmiths made their jewellery from melted Spanish, Argentine and Chilean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins, and old pendants that still hold whole coins help researchers to date them.</p></aside>
    </section>

